CHAPTERS = [
dict(title="Venice by Lamplight", scene="canal", paras=[
"Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive. Tonight we are going to drift, very slowly, through a city built on water. You do not need to follow every detail, and you do not need to stay awake until the end. Think of this as a long, unhurried ride in a small boat. The water will hold you up, and if you fall asleep along the way, the boat will simply keep gliding on.",
"Let your body settle wherever you are lying. Let your jaw loosen, and your forehead soften. Take one slow breath in, and let it out as gently as an oar slips back into calm water. Then take another, and let that one go too.",
"The year is somewhere in the later fifteen hundreds, in the Republic of Venice. It is an evening in early autumn. The warm days are shortening, the air over the lagoon is mild and damp, and the bells of the city's churches have just finished ringing for the end of the day. Lamps are being lit in windows, one after another, all along the canals.",
"For the rest of tonight, we will float from one quiet corner of the city to the next. We will sit in a gondola, rest beside an old well, cross a crowded wooden bridge that is slowly emptying, and look out across the lagoon at distant fires on a little island of glassmakers. And somewhere along the way, I hope, the water will rock you to sleep.",
]),

dict(title="A City Standing in the Sea", scene="lagoon", paras=[
"Venice sits in the middle of a wide, shallow lagoon, a great sheet of salt water sheltered from the open Adriatic by long, low strips of sand. Out here, the water is rarely deeper than a person standing. Marsh grass grows on the mudbanks, and at low tide the flats shine like pewter under the evening sky.",
"The city itself is made of more than a hundred small islands, crowded so closely together that the narrow channels between them have become streets of water. Bridges join one island to the next, hundreds of them, most of them small humped arches of brick and stone, just wide enough for two people to pass.",
"Beneath every church and palace and ordinary house, hidden in the mud, there is a forest. Builders drove countless wooden piles, mostly oak and larch, deep into the soft ground, packed them tightly together, and laid platforms of timber and stone across the tops. Sealed away from the air, in the wet clay, the wood keeps for centuries. The whole city rests quietly on those buried trunks.",
"In this evening of the later fifteen hundreds, perhaps a hundred and seventy thousand people live here, which makes Venice one of the largest cities in Europe. Merchants, sailors, glassmakers, weavers, boatbuilders, priests, servants, and a great many children, all packed onto these little islands, with the tide rising and falling gently around their doorsteps twice a day.",
"There are no carts here, and no horses clattering through the lanes. Everything that is heavy travels by boat. Barrels of wine, sacks of flour, firewood, bricks, bales of wool, baskets of fish, all of it floats into the city on flat-bottomed barges, pushed along with long poles. So the sound of Venice in the evening is not the sound of wheels. It is the sound of water, and footsteps, and voices echoing off stone.",
"Tonight the lagoon is calm. A faint mist lies over the water, and the low line of the city seems to float on top of it. The bell towers rise out of the haze, one here, one there, and the light from a few hundred windows quivers on the surface below, broken into little golden pieces by the slow movement of the tide.",
"Near the edge of a mudbank, a fisherman in a small flat boat is drawing in his last net of the day. He works slowly, hand over hand, letting the water drain through the mesh, and lifting out a few silvery fish, which he lays in a basket at his feet. Then he coils the net neatly in the bottom of the boat, picks up his pole, and pushes off towards home, leaving a long, slow ripple behind him.",
"Out on the water, rows of wooden posts mark the safe channels through the shallows. They lean a little, in pairs and threes, tied together at the top, dark against the pale water. A heron stands beside one of them, perfectly still, on one long leg, watching the surface. It has been standing there for a long time, and it is in no hurry at all.",
"Beyond the city, to the east, a long, thin island of sand and pine trees lies between the lagoon and the open sea. On a still night like this one, if you listen very carefully, you can just hear the sea on the far side of it, a slow hush, and then another, like someone breathing in a deep and peaceful sleep. The lagoon itself makes almost no sound at all.",
]),

dict(title="The Gondola", scene="canal", paras=[
"At the foot of a small stone landing, a gondola is waiting for you. It is long and narrow, about as long as five people lying head to foot, and it sits very low in the water. It is painted entirely black, and it gleams softly, like a polished shoe, in the lamplight.",
"Not so long ago, the gondolas of Venice were painted in every colour, with gilded carvings and bright cloth and silk cushions, each family trying to outshine the next. In fifteen sixty two, the Republic passed a law against such displays of wealth on the water, and from then on, the gondolas were made black. Some people complained. But on an evening like this, the plain dark boats look calm and dignified, gliding along like long, quiet shadows.",
"Look closely, and you will see that the gondola is not quite straight. One side is built a little fuller than the other, so that the boat leans and balances against the push of a single oar. At the front, a tall, flat blade of iron rises from the prow, catching the light. In the middle, there is a small cabin, a little wooden frame covered in black cloth, with tiny windows and a low doorway.",
"Step down carefully into the boat. It dips, and sways, and then steadies beneath you. Duck through the low doorway into the cabin and settle onto the cushions. Inside, it is snug and dim, and smells faintly of wool and wood and the sea. Through the little window, you can see the lamps of the landing, and the dark water moving softly beside you.",
"Your gondolier stands at the stern, behind you, high above the water. He rows standing up, facing forward, with one long oar resting in a carved wooden oarlock, a curved and twisted piece of walnut that lets him push, and turn, and slow, all with the same oar. He leans into the first stroke, and the landing slides quietly away.",
"Boats like this one are built by hand in small boatyards called squeri, tucked beside the canals, where the ground slopes gently down to the water. There, beneath wide wooden roofs, the builders shape each gondola from several different kinds of timber, oak and larch, elm and walnut, cherry and lime, each one chosen for its own particular job. A good gondola takes many weeks to make, and the builders know every curve of it by heart.",
"There is almost no sound. Just the soft dip of the oar, a little swirl of water, a pause, and then the dip again. The gondola glides forward between the tall walls of the houses, and the lamplight from the windows passes slowly over the black cloth of your little cabin, one window, then another, then another.",
"At a corner where two canals meet, the gondolier calls out a single word, long and low, so that any boat coming the other way will hear him before it sees him. Somewhere beyond the corner, another voice answers. The two boats pass each other with an arm's length to spare, and then the water is quiet again.",
"Out on the wider water, you pass one of the ferry stations, a little wooden landing where a few plain boats wait to carry people from one bank to the other for a small coin. A woman with a basket stands in one of them, perfectly balanced, as it crosses the canal, and a dog sits patiently at her feet, looking out at the lights. In a few moments, they are on the far side, and the ferryman turns back to wait again.",
]),

dict(title="Wellheads and Little Squares", scene="campo", paras=[
"The gondola bumps gently against a step, and you climb out onto the stones of a small square. In Venice, the open squares are not called piazzas. There is only one piazza, the great square in front of the church of Saint Mark. Every other square, large or small, is called a campo, a field, because long ago many of them really were grassy fields, with gardens and orchards and grazing animals.",
"This campo is paved now, with brick laid in a herringbone pattern, and it is ringed by houses with shutters and small balconies. On one side stands a parish church, with a tall bell tower of rosy brick. On the other side, an old woman is sitting on a doorstep, enjoying the last of the mild evening air, with a shawl over her shoulders.",
"In the middle of the square, there is a wellhead. It is a heavy round drum of carved stone, about waist high, decorated with leaves and shields and a worn coat of arms. Its rim has been rubbed smooth by generations of ropes and buckets and resting hands. An iron lid covers the opening for the night.",
"It is strange, when you think about it, a city surrounded on every side by water, and yet thirsty for it. The lagoon is salt. So Venice drinks the rain. Under almost every campo there is a hidden cistern, a great pit lined with thick clay to keep the sea out, and filled with clean sand.",
"Look down at the paving, and you will notice that the square slopes very gently towards a few small stone grates. When it rains, the water runs over the bricks, slips through those grates, and soaks slowly down through the sand, which filters it. At the centre, a shaft of brick gathers the clean water, and the wellhead sits on top of that shaft, like a lid on a jar.",
"In many squares, the iron lid on the well is locked at night, and someone from the parish keeps the key. Water is precious here, and it is shared carefully. In the morning, at the proper hour, the lid will be opened, and the buckets will go down, one after another, on their long ropes, and come back up dripping and cold.",
"In dry summers, when the cisterns run low, boatmen bring fresh water across the lagoon from a river on the mainland, in broad, low barges, and pour it carefully into the wells. But tonight the cistern is full. It rained two days ago, and the sand beneath your feet is still holding the cool, sweet water, slowly, patiently, out of sight.",
"In the morning, this square will be full of noise. Women with copper buckets on a yoke across their shoulders, talking and laughing as they wait their turn at the well. Children chasing each other around it. A man selling chestnuts. But for now, there is only the old woman on her doorstep, and a cat walking slowly along the rim of the well, and above it all, the bell tower, dark against a sky full of quiet stars.",
"Up on the rooftops around the campo, you can see small wooden platforms built on top of the houses, like little decks perched among the chimneys. They are called altane. In the daytime, people climb up to them to hang their washing, or to sit in the sun and the breeze, high above the narrow lanes. Tonight, a few white sheets are still pegged out on one of them, stirring very slowly in the mild night air.",
]),

dict(title="The Wooden Bridge at Rialto", scene="rialto", paras=[
"Now we walk a little way through the lanes, to the busiest place in all of Venice. Here, the Grand Canal, the great curving waterway that winds through the middle of the city like a backwards letter S, is crossed by a single bridge. The bridge at Rialto.",
"Tonight's bridge is made of wood. There have been several wooden bridges here over the centuries. One was damaged during an uprising, long ago. Another collapsed in fourteen forty four, under the weight of a crowd that had gathered on it to watch a grand procession of boats. Each time, the Venetians patiently built it again.",
"This one is a long, heavy timber bridge, with two rows of little shops running along its sides, their shutters closed for the night. In the very middle, the bridge rises higher, and the central section was made so that it could be raised, to let tall-masted ships pass through. You can find this very bridge, painted in loving detail, in pictures by the Venetian painter Carpaccio, with its shops and its crowds, and gondolas sliding beneath it.",
"For some years now, people in the city have been talking about replacing it with a bridge of stone. Famous architects have drawn designs. Councils have met, and argued, and met again. It will be some years yet before the white stone bridge that we know today is finished. For now, the old wooden one creaks softly underfoot, and smells of tar and damp timber.",
"Around the foot of the bridge lies the great market of Rialto. During the day, it is a roar of voices. Fishmongers calling out their catch. Greengrocers stacking melons and figs and baskets of herbs. Bankers sitting at their tables beneath the arcades, writing in thick ledgers. Merchants from every corner of the Mediterranean, arguing over the price of pepper and silk.",
"Near the bridge, there is a small church, so old that the story goes it was founded on the very day the city itself began, more than a thousand years earlier. In the little square in front of it stands a stone platform with a short flight of steps, held up by a crouching stone figure, a small, bent man carrying the weight on his back. From the top of those steps, the Republic's officials read out new laws and announcements to the crowd.",
"But it is evening now, and the market is closing. The fish stalls have been washed down with buckets of lagoon water, and the stones are wet and shining. A man sweeps cabbage leaves into a heap. Two boys are carrying a long pole between them, with empty baskets swinging from it. The last barges are being poled away, low in the water, towards the islands where they belong.",
"Just along the canal stands a great warehouse of the German merchants, with a courtyard in the middle and painted walls facing the water. On those walls, two young painters, Giorgione and Titian, once painted enormous figures, many years ago. In the fading light you can only just make out the colours, soft reds and golds, already a little weathered by the sea air.",
"Stand for a moment at the top of the bridge, and look along the Grand Canal. Palaces line both banks, their stone balconies and pointed windows reflected upside down in the water. Lanterns hang by the water gates. Gondolas glide slowly up and down, each with a small light at the bow. And the whole long curve of the canal glimmers, softly, all the way to the next bend.",
"Beneath your feet, a gondola passes under the bridge, slow and silent. For a moment, you can see the small lantern at its bow shining up on the wet timbers of the arch, and then it slides out on the other side, and carries on down the canal, growing smaller and smaller, until it is just one more little light among all the others on the water.",
]),

dict(title="Small Lights in the Lanes", scene="campo", paras=[
"Away from the Grand Canal, the lanes of Venice are narrow and winding. Some of them are so narrow that you could touch the walls on both sides at once, just by stretching out your arms. They twist, and turn, and cross little bridges, and sometimes they end suddenly at the edge of a canal, with nothing but water beyond.",
"After dark, these lanes would be very black indeed, if not for small lights. Centuries ago, the story goes, a Doge ordered little oil lamps to be hung on the walls of the darkest corners, to make the city safer at night. They were tiny things, hardly more than a cup of oil and a floating wick, but each one made a small, warm circle in the dark.",
"Many of the lights in the lanes are set before small shrines. Here and there, on a corner, there is a little painted image of the Madonna, framed in carved stone or wood, sometimes with a small roof above it to keep off the rain. In front of it, a lamp burns, and sometimes a few flowers have been left in a jar. Neighbours take turns to keep the lamp filled with oil, so that it burns all night long.",
"There is a gentle legend in Venice about one such light. The story goes that, many years ago, a young baker was wrongly accused of a crime and punished for it, and only afterwards was his innocence discovered. Ever since, it is said, a lamp has been kept burning high on the side of the great church of Saint Mark, in his memory, and as a reminder to judges to think carefully before they decide.",
"People who go out at night are expected to carry a light of their own. Wealthy households send a servant walking ahead with a lantern, its glass panels throwing soft yellow shapes on the walls. Ordinary folk carry a small lamp, or a candle in a little box, cupped carefully in one hand. All through the city, small lights are moving, slowly, along the lanes and over the bridges.",
"Follow one of them for a while. A man in a long dark cloak, walking home from his work. His lantern swings gently, and his shadow stretches out along the wall, then shrinks, then stretches again. He crosses a little bridge, nods to a neighbour on a balcony above, and turns down a passage so narrow that his lantern almost brushes the bricks.",
"Here and there, a lane ducks right underneath a house, through a low, dark passage with wooden beams overhead, where the upper floors of the building simply carry on above you. The Venetians call these covered passages sotoporteghi. Walking through one at night is like passing through a short tunnel, with the sound of your footsteps close around you, and then out again, into the open air, beside the soft gleam of a canal.",
"At the end of the passage, a door opens. Warm light spills out onto the stones. You hear the clink of a pot, a child's sleepy voice, and someone laughing softly. Then the door closes, and the passage is quiet again, lit only by the steady little flame before the shrine on the corner.",
]),

dict(title="The Arsenal Sleeps", scene="lagoon", paras=[
"At the eastern end of the city, behind high brick walls with battlements along the top, lies the Arsenal, the great shipyard of the Republic. During the day, it is the busiest place in Venice after the market. Thousands of workers, carpenters, caulkers, rope makers, sail makers, smiths, all labouring together to build the ships that carry Venetian trade across the sea.",
"Every working morning, a great bell in the tower of Saint Mark calls them to the yard. It is the deepest bell of all, and the Venetians gave it a name of its own, the Marangona, which comes from their word for a carpenter. It rings again in the evening, telling them that the day's work is done.",
"Inside the walls, there are long rows of covered sheds, with tall roofs on wooden pillars, standing side by side along the water. The ships were built in stages, moving from one place to the next, so that a hull might arrive at one shed and leave it a little more complete, while stores of oars, masts, and fittings waited, ready, in great warehouses.",
"Along one side of the yard stands a building that seems to go on for ever, a very long, narrow hall, where the rope makers work. To make the great ropes that a ship needs, they walk slowly backwards down the length of the hall, twisting strands of hemp as they go, again and again, until each rope is long and thick and strong. Tonight, the long hall is empty, and smells faintly of hemp and tar.",
"The story goes that when a young king of France came to visit Venice, he was taken to see the Arsenal. Before he sat down to a grand banquet, he watched the workers begin assembling a galley. By the time the meal was finished, the ship was complete, and ready to be launched. The tale may have grown a little in the telling, as good tales do. But it shows how proud the Venetians were of their great yard.",
"Long ago, the poet Dante saw this place, and remembered the workers boiling sticky pitch in great cauldrons, to seal the seams of the ships. In the daytime, the air still smells of it, warm and dark and resinous, drifting out over the water.",
"The workers of the Arsenal were proud of their craft, and the Republic honoured them for it. On great feast days, it was the men of the Arsenal who rowed the Doge's magnificent ceremonial barge, all red and gold, out across the lagoon, with banners flying and music playing. But tonight they are at home, in the little streets near the yard, asleep beside their families.",
"Tonight, all of that is still. The gates are closed. The sheds stand silent in long rows, their tall roofs pale under the moon. A watchman walks slowly along the top of the wall, carrying a lantern, and then stops for a while, leaning on the parapet, looking out at the calm water. Somewhere inside, a half-built hull rests on its timbers, waiting for the morning.",
"Across the water from the Arsenal, on a small island of its own, a new church is slowly rising, designed by the architect Palladio, all calm white columns and rounded arches. Tonight, it is wrapped in scaffolding, and the moonlight falls softly on the half-finished stone. It will take many years to complete. But it has time. It is in no hurry, and neither are we.",
]),

dict(title="The Glow from Murano", scene="canal", paras=[
"The gondola carries you north now, through a long, quiet canal lined with the backs of houses, past small gardens behind high walls, where fig trees lean over the brickwork and a few late roses are still blooming. The gondolier's oar dips, and lifts, and dips again, and the water whispers along the sides of the boat.",
"The houses open out, and suddenly you are at the edge of the lagoon again, looking north across the dark water. And there, a little way off, on a cluster of low islands, you can see a faint orange glow. It flickers, very softly, on the undersides of the clouds. That is Murano, the island of the glassmakers.",
"On the way, you pass a small island with a walled monastery and a pale stone church, its front gleaming softly in the moonlight. The water around it is perfectly smooth, and the reflections of its walls and of a few tall trees stand upside down beneath them, so that the whole little island seems to have a twin, reaching down into the lagoon.",
"Long ago, in twelve ninety one, the Republic ordered almost all the glass furnaces in Venice to be moved out to Murano. A city built so tightly, with so much timber in its roofs and floors and foundations, was terrified of fire, and glass furnaces burn very hot indeed. So the glassmakers moved across the water, and Murano became a little world of its own.",
"Inside the workshops, the furnaces are domes of brick, glowing at their mouths like small suns. The glassmakers sit on wooden benches beside them, turning long iron pipes in their hands. On the end of each pipe, a glowing blob of molten glass slowly swells, and stretches, and takes shape, a goblet, a bowl, a delicate little vase, as the maker breathes into the pipe and rolls it along the arms of his bench.",
"Venetian glass was famous everywhere. In the previous century, the glassmakers here had learned to make a glass so clear and colourless that it was called cristallo, as if it were rock crystal. Fine tables in every great city of Europe wanted Venetian glasses, Venetian mirrors, and Venetian beads, and so the master glassmakers of Murano were treasured, and protected, and closely watched.",
"Murano is not only furnaces and workshops. It is also a quiet place of gardens and orchards, of small canals and old churches, and some of the noble families of Venice keep summer houses there, with shady loggias and long garden walks, where they come to escape the crowded city. On an autumn night, the gardens are still, and smell of damp earth and fallen leaves.",
"From here, across the water, you cannot hear the workshops at all. You can only see the glow, warm and steady, a soft amber smudge on the night. Imagine, in one of those workshops, a young apprentice, his work finished, sitting by the cooling furnace, watching the last of the coals turn from orange to deep red. His eyes are heavy. His head is beginning to nod.",
"The apprentice wraps himself in his cloak and lies down on a pallet of straw in the corner, where the brick of the furnace wall still gives off a gentle warmth. Outside, the canal laps softly against the steps. He listens to it for a little while, and to the faint creak of a boat at its mooring, and to the slow ticking of the cooling glass on the shelves. And then he is asleep.",
"The gondolier rests on his oar for a while, and lets the boat drift. Small waves lap against the black hull. The glow from Murano trembles on the water in a long, broken line, reaching almost all the way to the boat. And everything is very quiet.",
]),

dict(title="The Lagoon at Midnight", scene="lagoon", paras=[
"Slowly, the gondola turns back towards the city. The gondolier is in no hurry. Neither are you. The lamps along the canals are fewer now. Many of the windows have gone dark, one by one, as families pinch out their candles and climb into bed.",
"Far off, from the tower of Saint Mark, a bell rings out over the water, slow and deep. It is late. In the old days of the Republic, a bell like this told the city that the night had truly begun, that it was time for the taverns to close and for the streets to empty and for everyone to go home.",
"Imagine the whole city settling. In a palace on the Grand Canal, a merchant closes his ledger and blows out his lamp. In a small house beside a campo, a mother tucks a woollen blanket around three sleeping children. In the Arsenal, the watchman finishes his round and sits down by a small brazier, warming his hands.",
"In a monastery on one of the little islands, a single monk is still awake, reading by a candle, his finger moving slowly along the lines of a heavy book. After a while, his eyes close, and open, and close again. He marks his page with a ribbon, pinches out the flame, and the island goes dark, like all the others.",
"Out on the lagoon, the mist is thickening a little, soft and white, curling low over the water. The mooring posts stand in it like quiet figures. The heron has gone to sleep, somewhere in the reeds. Only the tide moves now, slowly, patiently, as it has moved here twice a day for thousands of years, washing in and washing out, around the stones of the sleeping city.",
"Lean back against the cushions. Let the gentle sway of the boat move through you. The oar dips, and rises, and dips again. The lamplight slides slowly past the little window, warm, and then gone, and then warm again. You do not need to watch where we are going. The gondolier knows the way.",
"Breathe slowly with the rhythm of the oar. In, as the blade lifts from the water. Out, as it slides back in. In, and out. The boat moves forward a little with each breath, and the stone walls of the city drift past, soft and grey and silent, and the water carries you on.",
"Soon the boat will slip back into the canals, and glide beneath the little bridges, and come to rest at a quiet landing beside a door with a lamp above it. But you do not need to wait for that. You can let the gondola carry you the rest of the way while you sleep.",
"Thank you for floating through Venice with The Quiet Archive tonight. Let the water hold you, and let the night be long and soft and calm. Sleep well. Goodnight.",
]),
]
